## 🏛️ IT Governance, Risk Assessment & Compliance

This lab was not only built as a technical exercise — it was operated under a structured IT governance framework reflecting the standards expected in German enterprise environments. Every decision made during setup and daily operations was evaluated against governance, risk, and compliance (GRC) principles.

---

### 🏛️ IT Governance Framework

IT governance in this lab follows the **ITIL v4 Service Management** framework, ensuring that IT services are aligned with business needs, delivered consistently, and improved continuously.

**Governance principles applied:**

| Principle | Implementation in this Lab |
|-----------|---------------------------|
| **Focus on value** | Every ticket tier (L1/L2/L3) is mapped to business impact — VPN outages affecting 12 users are treated as higher priority than single-user issues |
| **Start where you are** | Existing Ubuntu infrastructure was assessed before installing new services rather than rebuilding from scratch |
| **Progress iteratively** | Lab was built in 5 structured phases, each validated before proceeding to the next |
| **Collaborate and promote visibility** | All ticket actions, escalations, and resolutions are documented internally — full audit trail maintained |
| **Think and work holistically** | Mail stack (Postfix + Dovecot), ticketing (Zammad), security (Fail2ban + TLS), and automation (scripts + cron) are treated as one integrated system |
| **Keep it simple** | Automation triggers use clear keyword-based routing logic; scripts are readable and commented |
| **Optimise and automate** | SLA monitoring, backup, retention, and ticket routing are all automated — reducing manual intervention |

**Change Management** was applied throughout the lab. Every configuration change to Postfix, Dovecot, or Zammad was tested in isolation before being committed, and every change is traceable through ticket notes and script history.

