### 🔐 LINDDUN Privacy Threat Model

LINDDUN was applied to identify privacy threats specific to the personal data processed by the ticketing system. This is particularly important under DSGVO, where personal data in support tickets (names, email addresses, employee IDs, device information) must be protected throughout its lifecycle.

**LINDDUN Categories:**
- **L** — Linkability
- **I** — Identifiability
- **N** — Non-repudiation
- **D** — Detectability
- **D** — Disclosure of information
- **U** — Unawareness
- **N** — Non-compliance

#### Personal Data Assets in Scope

| Data Asset | Where Stored | Personal Data Elements |
|-----------|-------------|----------------------|
| Ticket records | Zammad PostgreSQL | Customer name, email, issue description, device info |
| Mail messages | Dovecot mailbox | Sender name, email address, message content |
| User accounts | Zammad PostgreSQL | Agent name, login email, role assignments |
| Backup files | `/var/backups/zammad/` | All of the above in compressed DB dump |
| Log files | `/var/log/mail.log`, `/var/log/zammad/` | IP addresses, email addresses, login attempts |

#### LINDDUN Threat Analysis

| Threat ID | Category | Description | Privacy Impact | Mitigation Applied |
|-----------|----------|-------------|---------------|-------------------|
| L-01 | **Linkability** | Tickets from the same customer across multiple incidents can be linked to build a behavioural profile | Medium | Data minimisation limits what is stored per ticket |
| L-02 | **Linkability** | Mail logs link IP addresses to email addresses and timestamps | Medium | Logs not retained beyond operational necessity |
| I-01 | **Identifiability** | Employee ID numbers in ticket notes directly identify individuals | High | Employee IDs used only for verification; not stored in searchable fields |
| I-02 | **Identifiability** | Agent names visible in all ticket notes and audit logs | Low | Accepted as necessary for accountability; access restricted to admin |
| N-01 | **Non-repudiation** | All admin actions are logged and attributed — admins cannot deny actions taken | Low risk | Activity Stream provides full audit trail; satisfies DSGVO Art. 5(2) |
| D-01 | **Detectability** | Network observer can detect ticket creation patterns from SMTP volume | Low | TLS encrypts content; metadata patterns unavoidable at network level |
| D-02 | **Detectability** | Fail2ban logs reveal which IPs attempted to access which services | Low | Log access restricted to root; purge schedule in place |
| DD-01 | **Disclosure** | Backup files contain full personal data — if stolen, all data exposed | Critical | Root-only access to backup directory; production recommendation: encrypt at rest |
| DD-02 | **Disclosure** | Zammad API returns full user objects including email and preferences | High | API tokens scoped to minimum permissions; HTTPS recommended for production |
| U-01 | **Unawareness** | Customers submitting tickets may not know retention period or who has access | High | Privacy notice recommended for production deployment |
| U-02 | **Unawareness** | Auto-created customer accounts may not be aware their email was used | Medium | Auto-account creation is a Zammad default; explicit consent needed in production |
| NC-01 | **Non-compliance** | Retaining ticket data indefinitely violates DSGVO Art. 5(1)(e) | Critical | `zammad-retention.sh` auto-closes tickets after 90 days; backups purged after 30 days |
| NC-02 | **Non-compliance** | No documented lawful basis for processing employee support data | High | In production: legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f)) documented in RoPA |

#### LINDDUN Mitigations Summary

| Priority | Mitigation | Status |
|---------|-----------|--------|
| 🔴 Critical | Encrypt backup files at rest | ⚠️ Recommended for production |
| 🔴 Critical | DSGVO-compliant retention enforced (90-day auto-close) | ✅ Implemented |
| 🟠 High | Publish privacy notice for ticket submitters | ⚠️ Recommended for production |
| 🟠 High | Enforce HTTPS on Zammad web interface | ⚠️ Recommended for production |
| 🟠 High | Document lawful basis for processing in RoPA | ⚠️ Recommended for production |
| 🟡 Medium | Review auto-account creation behaviour | ⚠️ Configuration review needed |
| 🟡 Medium | Minimise personal data in ticket notes | ✅ Applied in resolution documentation |
| 🟢 Low | Review log retention periods | ✅ Logs monitored; purge schedule in place |

